1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,489
A build that waits for someone to click is not continuous integration.

2
00:00:05,039 --> 00:00:07,575
Today three things happen on their own.

3
00:00:07,575 --> 00:00:09,721
A build starts when code changes.

4
00:00:09,721 --> 00:00:11,411
SonarQube judges the code,

5
00:00:11,411 --> 00:00:12,907
and can stop the build.

6
00:00:12,907 --> 00:00:15,378
And Nexus stores the finished release,

7
00:00:15,378 --> 00:00:17,263
so nobody ever overwrites it.

8
00:00:17,813 --> 00:00:19,451
You will see all three,

9
00:00:19,451 --> 00:00:21,729
on real servers: a real Jenkins,

10
00:00:21,729 --> 00:00:22,939
a real SonarQube,

11
00:00:22,939 --> 00:00:24,149
and a real Nexus.

12
00:00:24,699 --> 00:00:28,966
And you will watch a bad commit get stopped by the quality gate, before it ships.

13
00:00:30,516 --> 00:00:31,945
Here is what to expect.

14
00:00:32,495 --> 00:00:35,013
One: the ways a build can start by itself.

15
00:00:35,563 --> 00:00:38,912
Two: the road from commit to release, and where the gate sits.

16
00:00:39,462 --> 00:00:40,782
Three: a live demo,

17
00:00:40,782 --> 00:00:42,172
in three real tools.

18
00:00:42,172 --> 00:00:44,187
Polling and a remote trigger.

19
00:00:44,187 --> 00:00:45,993
A quality gate that fails,

20
00:00:45,993 --> 00:00:46,827
then passes.

21
00:00:46,827 --> 00:00:48,425
And a publish to Nexus,

22
00:00:48,425 --> 00:00:49,467
with a refusal.

23
00:00:50,017 --> 00:00:52,833
Four: the common mistakes, and where to find the lab.

24
00:00:53,383 --> 00:00:56,135
This is objective six of the Jenkins domain.

25
00:00:58,185 --> 00:00:59,934
How does a build start by itself?

26
00:01:00,484 --> 00:01:02,358
Jenkins has four main ways.

27
00:01:02,358 --> 00:01:04,163
One: poll the source code.

28
00:01:04,163 --> 00:01:05,343
Jenkins asks git,

29
00:01:05,343 --> 00:01:06,592
every few minutes,

30
00:01:06,592 --> 00:01:07,980
if anything changed.

31
00:01:07,980 --> 00:01:08,882
It is simple,

32
00:01:08,882 --> 00:01:11,172
and it works behind any firewall.

33
00:01:11,722 --> 00:01:12,682
Two: a webhook.

34
00:01:12,682 --> 00:01:16,458
The git server calls a Jenkins address the moment you push.

35
00:01:16,458 --> 00:01:17,290
It is faster,

36
00:01:17,290 --> 00:01:19,914
and it asks nothing when nothing changed.

37
00:01:20,464 --> 00:01:24,880
Three: a timer. Build every night, whatever happened. Good for slow checks.

38
00:01:25,430 --> 00:01:29,249
Four: another job. When one build finishes, the next one starts.

39
00:01:29,799 --> 00:01:34,151
In the demo we use polling, and a remote trigger, which is how a webhook works.

40
00:01:35,701 --> 00:01:37,578
Now the road from commit to release.

41
00:01:38,128 --> 00:01:41,542
A developer commits. Jenkins builds, and runs the tests.

42
00:01:42,092 --> 00:01:44,314
Then SonarQube analyses the code.

43
00:01:44,314 --> 00:01:45,526
It looks for bugs,

44
00:01:45,526 --> 00:01:48,961
security problems and smells that tests do not see.

45
00:01:49,511 --> 00:01:52,181
The quality gate is a pass or fail rule.

46
00:01:52,181 --> 00:01:53,849
For example: no new bugs.

47
00:01:53,849 --> 00:01:54,650
If it fails,

48
00:01:54,650 --> 00:01:56,252
the pipeline stops here.

49
00:01:56,802 --> 00:02:01,901
Only a build that passes the gate is published to Nexus, the artifact repository.

50
00:02:02,451 --> 00:02:04,524
A release in Nexus is immutable.

51
00:02:04,524 --> 00:02:07,439
The same version can never be uploaded twice.

52
00:02:07,439 --> 00:02:08,605
That is a feature,

53
00:02:08,605 --> 00:02:09,512
not a problem.

54
00:02:11,062 --> 00:02:16,587
A real Jenkins, again. I sign in as admin. We start with the simplest trigger, polling.

55
00:02:23,137 --> 00:02:25,975
New Item. A Pipeline, named CloudCart poll.

56
00:02:26,525 --> 00:02:28,762
Scroll down to Build Triggers.

57
00:02:28,762 --> 00:02:30,328
Here are the choices.

58
00:02:30,328 --> 00:02:32,118
Trigger builds remotely.

59
00:02:32,118 --> 00:02:33,535
Build periodically.

60
00:02:33,535 --> 00:02:34,654
And Poll SCM.

61
00:02:34,654 --> 00:02:35,997
I tick Poll SCM.

62
00:02:36,547 --> 00:02:38,465
The schedule is five stars.

63
00:02:38,465 --> 00:02:38,963
Minute,

64
00:02:38,963 --> 00:02:39,318
hour,

65
00:02:39,318 --> 00:02:39,602
day,

66
00:02:39,602 --> 00:02:40,029
month,

67
00:02:40,029 --> 00:02:40,597
weekday.

68
00:02:40,597 --> 00:02:42,729
Five stars means every minute.

69
00:02:42,729 --> 00:02:43,653
In real life,

70
00:02:43,653 --> 00:02:46,637
five minutes is kinder to your git server.

71
00:02:47,187 --> 00:02:47,539
Next,

72
00:02:47,539 --> 00:02:49,228
Trigger builds remotely.

73
00:02:49,228 --> 00:02:49,932
I tick it,

74
00:02:49,932 --> 00:02:51,340
and give it a token,

75
00:02:51,340 --> 00:02:52,044
lab token.

76
00:02:52,044 --> 00:02:56,126
Now this job has an address that outside systems can call.

77
00:02:56,676 --> 00:02:58,447
In the Pipeline section,

78
00:02:58,447 --> 00:03:00,587
the definition is from SCM.

79
00:03:00,587 --> 00:03:00,883
Git,

80
00:03:00,883 --> 00:03:02,359
the repository path,

81
00:03:02,359 --> 00:03:03,466
and the branch,

82
00:03:03,466 --> 00:03:03,835
main.

83
00:03:03,835 --> 00:03:07,230
The Jenkinsfile is already in the repository.

84
00:03:07,230 --> 00:03:07,599
Save.

85
00:03:08,149 --> 00:03:08,809
Build Now,

86
00:03:08,809 --> 00:03:09,139
once,

87
00:03:09,139 --> 00:03:11,319
to give polling a starting point.

88
00:03:11,319 --> 00:03:14,422
While it runs: the build clones the repository,

89
00:03:14,422 --> 00:03:15,611
compiles the code,

90
00:03:15,611 --> 00:03:17,130
and runs all the tests.

91
00:03:37,680 --> 00:03:38,278
Now Dana,

92
00:03:38,278 --> 00:03:39,074
a developer,

93
00:03:39,074 --> 00:03:40,535
pushes a small commit.

94
00:03:40,535 --> 00:03:41,930
A note in the readme.

95
00:03:41,930 --> 00:03:43,523
I do nothing in Jenkins.

96
00:03:43,523 --> 00:03:44,918
Watch the build list.

97
00:03:44,918 --> 00:03:45,980
Within a minute,

98
00:03:45,980 --> 00:03:47,640
build number two appears,

99
00:03:47,640 --> 00:03:48,304
by itself.

100
00:04:28,854 --> 00:04:29,963
Open the console.

101
00:04:29,963 --> 00:04:33,811
The first lines tell the cause: started by an SCM change.

102
00:04:33,811 --> 00:04:35,702
Polling found the new commit.

103
00:04:36,252 --> 00:04:37,505
Now the faster way.

104
00:04:37,505 --> 00:04:40,077
A git server calls the trigger address,

105
00:04:40,077 --> 00:04:41,396
the moment you push.

106
00:04:41,396 --> 00:04:43,177
I open that address myself,

107
00:04:43,177 --> 00:04:44,562
with the token in it,

108
00:04:44,562 --> 00:04:46,540
exactly as a git server would.

109
00:04:46,540 --> 00:04:47,793
Build three starts.

110
00:04:58,343 --> 00:05:01,399
The cause is different: started by remote host.

111
00:05:01,399 --> 00:05:01,985
Same job,

112
00:05:01,985 --> 00:05:03,285
a different trigger.

113
00:05:03,285 --> 00:05:05,171
Polling asks again and again.

114
00:05:05,171 --> 00:05:07,837
A webhook speaks only when there is news.

115
00:05:08,387 --> 00:05:14,445
Now quality. SonarQube is a second real server, here on port nine thousand. I sign in.

116
00:05:34,995 --> 00:05:36,922
The home page lists projects.

117
00:05:36,922 --> 00:05:37,719
Our project,

118
00:05:37,719 --> 00:05:38,449
CloudCart,

119
00:05:38,449 --> 00:05:39,047
is empty.

120
00:05:39,047 --> 00:05:40,841
It has never been analysed.

121
00:05:51,391 --> 00:05:52,668
Open Quality Gates.

122
00:05:52,668 --> 00:05:55,022
Our gate is called CloudCart gate.

123
00:05:55,022 --> 00:05:57,510
One condition: new bugs must be zero.

124
00:05:57,510 --> 00:05:58,452
Remember that.

125
00:06:09,002 --> 00:06:10,127
Back to Jenkins.

126
00:06:10,127 --> 00:06:11,182
A new Pipeline,

127
00:06:11,182 --> 00:06:12,941
named CloudCart quality.

128
00:06:12,941 --> 00:06:14,910
The script has three stages.

129
00:06:14,910 --> 00:06:15,543
Checkout.

130
00:06:15,543 --> 00:06:15,895
Test.

131
00:06:15,895 --> 00:06:17,302
And the Sonar stage,

132
00:06:17,302 --> 00:06:18,990
which runs the analysis,

133
00:06:18,990 --> 00:06:20,607
and waits for the gate.

134
00:06:27,157 --> 00:06:29,736
The Sonar token comes from credentials,

135
00:06:29,736 --> 00:06:31,587
the same way as last lesson.

136
00:06:31,587 --> 00:06:33,240
The script only names it.

137
00:06:33,240 --> 00:06:34,034
The setting,

138
00:06:34,034 --> 00:06:35,224
quality gate wait,

139
00:06:35,224 --> 00:06:37,803
makes the build stop if the gate fails.

140
00:06:38,353 --> 00:06:38,683
Save,

141
00:06:38,683 --> 00:06:39,609
and Build Now.

142
00:06:39,609 --> 00:06:41,395
This is the first analysis,

143
00:06:41,395 --> 00:06:43,181
so it becomes our baseline.

144
00:06:43,181 --> 00:06:44,769
It takes about a minute.

145
00:06:44,769 --> 00:06:47,018
The scanner reads every Java file,

146
00:06:47,018 --> 00:06:49,068
sends the results to SonarQube,

147
00:06:49,068 --> 00:06:50,854
and the server judges them.

148
00:07:06,404 --> 00:07:07,846
While we wait: a bug,

149
00:07:07,846 --> 00:07:08,464
in Sonar,

150
00:07:08,464 --> 00:07:10,592
is code that is probably wrong.

151
00:07:10,592 --> 00:07:11,278
Not style,

152
00:07:11,278 --> 00:07:11,965
not taste.

153
00:07:11,965 --> 00:07:12,377
Wrong.

154
00:07:12,377 --> 00:07:15,535
That is why a gate on new bugs is a fair rule.

155
00:08:01,085 --> 00:08:06,781
Open the console. The last lines say it: quality gate status, passed. The baseline is set.

156
00:08:07,331 --> 00:08:11,939
In SonarQube the project now has a dashboard, with the gate result, passed.

157
00:08:12,489 --> 00:08:17,673
Now Dana adds coupon validation, and pushes. The commit looks fine. Build Now.

158
00:09:23,223 --> 00:09:24,228
The tests pass.

159
00:09:24,228 --> 00:09:25,502
The build compiles.

160
00:09:25,502 --> 00:09:28,518
But the console says it: quality gate status,

161
00:09:28,518 --> 00:09:28,987
failed.

162
00:09:28,987 --> 00:09:30,395
And the build is red.

163
00:09:30,395 --> 00:09:31,467
Jenkins stopped,

164
00:09:31,467 --> 00:09:33,143
because the gate said so.

165
00:09:33,693 --> 00:09:33,949
Why?

166
00:09:33,949 --> 00:09:34,783
In SonarQube,

167
00:09:34,783 --> 00:09:36,899
open the issues for the new code.

168
00:09:36,899 --> 00:09:37,668
One new bug.

169
00:09:37,668 --> 00:09:40,233
The code compares the value with itself,

170
00:09:40,233 --> 00:09:42,029
so the check can never fail.

171
00:09:42,029 --> 00:09:44,080
A test would not have caught it.

172
00:09:44,080 --> 00:09:44,914
The gate did.

173
00:09:45,464 --> 00:09:46,921
Dana fixes the check,

174
00:09:46,921 --> 00:09:47,685
and pushes.

175
00:09:47,685 --> 00:09:48,378
Build Now,

176
00:09:48,378 --> 00:09:48,795
again.

177
00:09:48,795 --> 00:09:50,391
The same analysis runs,

178
00:09:50,391 --> 00:09:51,779
with the fixed code.

179
00:11:04,329 --> 00:11:09,577
Quality gate status: passed. The build is green, and the bad code never reached a release.

180
00:11:10,127 --> 00:11:11,926
SonarQube has done its job.

181
00:11:11,926 --> 00:11:12,859
Now I stop it,

182
00:11:12,859 --> 00:11:14,924
to free memory on this machine,

183
00:11:14,924 --> 00:11:15,990
and start Nexus.

184
00:11:15,990 --> 00:11:18,123
Nexus is a big Java application,

185
00:11:18,123 --> 00:11:20,388
and needs about a minute to start.

186
00:11:20,388 --> 00:11:21,454
While it starts,

187
00:11:21,454 --> 00:11:23,453
a word about repository types.

188
00:11:23,453 --> 00:11:25,319
A hosted repository is ours,

189
00:11:25,319 --> 00:11:26,385
we upload to it.

190
00:11:26,385 --> 00:11:29,317
A proxy repository caches the outside world,

191
00:11:29,317 --> 00:11:30,583
like Maven Central.

192
00:11:30,583 --> 00:11:34,114
A group joins several repositories under one address.

193
00:11:34,114 --> 00:11:36,047
Today we use two hosted ones.

194
00:12:24,597 --> 00:12:31,082
Last tool: Nexus, a real artifact repository, here on port eighty eighty one. I sign in.

195
00:12:39,632 --> 00:12:40,491
Open Browse.

196
00:12:40,491 --> 00:12:45,213
Two hosted repositories of ours: corp releases and corp snapshots.

197
00:12:45,213 --> 00:12:46,858
Corp releases is empty.

198
00:12:46,858 --> 00:12:48,933
Releases there are immutable.

199
00:13:01,483 --> 00:13:02,669
Back to Jenkins.

200
00:13:02,669 --> 00:13:03,781
A new Pipeline,

201
00:13:03,781 --> 00:13:05,190
CloudCart publish.

202
00:13:05,190 --> 00:13:06,524
One stage deploys,

203
00:13:06,524 --> 00:13:07,339
with Maven.

204
00:13:07,339 --> 00:13:10,156
It uses the credential nexus deployer.

205
00:13:10,156 --> 00:13:11,861
That is a limited user,

206
00:13:11,861 --> 00:13:13,417
who can only publish.

207
00:13:13,417 --> 00:13:14,603
Never the admin.

208
00:13:15,153 --> 00:13:15,496
Save,

209
00:13:15,496 --> 00:13:16,455
and Build Now.

210
00:13:16,455 --> 00:13:18,168
Maven builds the package,

211
00:13:18,168 --> 00:13:19,813
and uploads it to Nexus.

212
00:13:19,813 --> 00:13:21,320
Each file is uploaded,

213
00:13:21,320 --> 00:13:22,416
the web archive,

214
00:13:22,416 --> 00:13:23,239
and the pom.

215
00:13:35,789 --> 00:13:36,877
Open the console.

216
00:13:36,877 --> 00:13:38,669
Look for the lines that say,

217
00:13:38,669 --> 00:13:39,757
uploaded to corp.

218
00:13:39,757 --> 00:13:42,445
Those are the files that arrived in Nexus.

219
00:13:42,995 --> 00:13:43,613
In Nexus,

220
00:13:43,613 --> 00:13:44,643
refresh Browse.

221
00:13:44,643 --> 00:13:45,604
Under academy,

222
00:13:45,604 --> 00:13:46,360
CloudCart,

223
00:13:46,360 --> 00:13:49,313
there is version zero point two point zero.

224
00:13:49,313 --> 00:13:50,824
The release is stored.

225
00:13:51,374 --> 00:13:54,489
Now the same build again, same version. Build Now.

226
00:14:05,039 --> 00:14:05,590
Refused.

227
00:14:05,590 --> 00:14:08,487
The message says: redeploy is not allowed.

228
00:14:08,487 --> 00:14:10,831
Nexus protects a released version.

229
00:14:10,831 --> 00:14:14,831
Nobody can silently replace what users already downloaded.

230
00:14:15,381 --> 00:14:17,080
The fix is a new version.

231
00:14:17,080 --> 00:14:18,643
Dana bumps the version,

232
00:14:18,643 --> 00:14:20,546
to zero point two point one,

233
00:14:20,546 --> 00:14:21,293
and pushes.

234
00:14:21,293 --> 00:14:21,973
Build Now.

235
00:14:32,523 --> 00:14:33,872
The build is green.

236
00:14:33,872 --> 00:14:34,511
In Nexus,

237
00:14:34,511 --> 00:14:39,837
version zero point two point one now sits beside zero point two point zero.

238
00:14:39,837 --> 00:14:41,328
Each release is kept,

239
00:14:41,328 --> 00:14:42,891
and each one is final.

240
00:14:43,441 --> 00:14:44,890
That is the whole road.

241
00:14:44,890 --> 00:14:45,520
A trigger,

242
00:14:45,520 --> 00:14:46,024
a build,

243
00:14:46,024 --> 00:14:46,465
a gate,

244
00:14:46,465 --> 00:14:47,347
and a release.

245
00:14:47,347 --> 00:14:48,545
Each step can fail,

246
00:14:48,545 --> 00:14:50,246
and each failure is useful.

247
00:14:51,796 --> 00:14:52,948
Five mistakes.

248
00:14:53,498 --> 00:14:57,786
One: polling every minute on a big server. Use five minutes, or a webhook.

249
00:14:58,336 --> 00:15:00,931
Two: a quality gate on the whole project.

250
00:15:00,931 --> 00:15:02,893
Old problems block every build.

251
00:15:02,893 --> 00:15:04,032
Gate the new code.

252
00:15:04,582 --> 00:15:09,915
Three: publishing before the gate. Always analyse first, and publish only what passed.

253
00:15:10,465 --> 00:15:15,095
Four: deploying releases as admin. Use a limited user, and a credential.

254
00:15:15,645 --> 00:15:19,805
Five: redeploying the same release version. Bump the version instead.

255
00:15:21,355 --> 00:15:24,150
Where to find the lab. Pause here, and write it down.

256
00:15:24,700 --> 00:15:26,164
One: the repository,

257
00:15:26,164 --> 00:15:30,484
git hub dot com slash it dash waves slash dev ops dash A I,

258
00:15:30,484 --> 00:15:32,828
in the folder academy slash lab.

259
00:15:33,378 --> 00:15:39,586
Two: the files, lab one fifty-nine dot S H, and shortcuts one fifty-nine dot S H.

260
00:15:40,136 --> 00:15:45,597
Three: install it, with sudo install, into slash user slash local slash S bin.

261
00:15:46,147 --> 00:15:48,012
Four: load the short names,

262
00:15:48,012 --> 00:15:51,119
with source shortcuts one fifty-nine dot S H.

263
00:15:51,119 --> 00:15:51,948
Then lab up,

264
00:15:51,948 --> 00:15:52,846
and lab repo.

265
00:15:53,396 --> 00:15:54,309
Five: lab bug,

266
00:15:54,309 --> 00:15:57,180
lab fix and lab bump play the three commits.

267
00:15:57,180 --> 00:16:00,052
Lab files writes the three pipeline scripts.

268
00:16:00,602 --> 00:16:04,339
Six: you need the Jenkins from lesson one fifty-five,

269
00:16:04,339 --> 00:16:05,185
a SonarQube,

270
00:16:05,185 --> 00:16:06,032
and a Nexus.

271
00:16:06,032 --> 00:16:08,218
Lab down removes the lab files.

272
00:16:08,768 --> 00:16:10,410
The link is in the description.

273
00:16:12,460 --> 00:16:14,231
Your turn, on your own machine.

274
00:16:14,781 --> 00:16:17,533
One: lab up, then lab repo, and read the scripts.

275
00:16:18,083 --> 00:16:21,560
Two: create the poll job, and watch a commit start a build.

276
00:16:22,110 --> 00:16:26,270
Three: run the quality pipeline, then lab bug, and see the gate fail.

277
00:16:26,820 --> 00:16:28,740
Four: lab fix, and see it pass.

278
00:16:29,290 --> 00:16:33,599
Five: publish to Nexus twice, and read the refusal. Then lab down.

279
00:16:34,149 --> 00:16:35,984
